\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 *No Meaningful Answer*}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Kantian, no access to \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91the thing in itself\u8217\'92}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Reality is fundamentally unknowable.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Not only don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t we know it, but we couldn\u8217\'92t express it if we did.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Science is nothing but a story (Postmodern)}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Reality is all in our head (constructivist)}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Reality doesn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t exist (solipsism)}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Horizon problem:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Alan guth: it couldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t have been a crazy fluke coincidence that infinitely many separate regions of space underwent Big Bang explosions all at once\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 somephysical mechanism jmust have caused both the exploding and the synchronizing. One unexplained big bang is bad enough; an infinitue number strains credulity.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Called the horizon problem because it involves what we see on our cosmic horizon, in the most distant regions we can observe. \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  uniformity of temperature, but not enough time for there to reach a uniform temperature via mixing. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Flatness problem.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve measured our space to be flat to high accruacy. Previous big bang models had flatness be unstable.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Inflation can create an infinte volume inside a finite volume. Specifically, it can start with something smaller than an atom and create an infinite space inside of it, containing infinitely many galaxies, without affecting the exterior space.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85Our physical space doesn\u8217\'92t come with centimeter marks built in the way a ruler does, nor does our universe come with a bunch of clocks pre-installed. Instead, any observer needs to define her own measurement rods and clocks, which in turn define her notion of space and time; eintstein\u8217\'92s relativity.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 - inflation produces an infinite number of galaxies by continuing forever. According to general relativity, an observer in one of these galaxies will view space and time differently, perceiving space as having been infinite already when inflation ended.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Our observable univers; horizon volume; region within our particle horizon; hubble volume - region within which galaxies are receding slower than light.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 If the cosmic expansion continues to accelerate (open question), the observable universe will eventually stop growing: all galaxies beyond a certain distance will eventually recede faster than light and forever be invisble to us.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s moutning evidence that our \u8216\'91empty space\u8217\'92 seems to be a sort of medium, with way more than the three phases of water\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  perhaps 10^500, and perhaps even infinitely many, which opens up the possibility that, in addition to curving, stretching and vibrating, our space may even be able to do something analogous to freezing and evaporationg.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Our cosmic history has been a gravitational tug of war between dark matter trying to pull things toegether and dark energy trying to push them apart. Because galaxy formation is all about pulling things together, I think of dark matter as our friend and dark energy as our enemy. The cosmic density used to be dominated by dark matter, and its friendly gravitational attraction helped assemble galaxies such as our own. However, because the cosmic expansion diluted the dark matter but not the dark energy, t he unkind gravitational repulsion of dark energy eventually gained the upper hand, sabotaging further galaxy formation. This means that if the dark energy had had significantly larger density, it would have started gaining the upper hand much sooner, before any galaxies had had time to form. The result would be a }{\f1\fs24\b1\i0\cf1 stillborn universe}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 , remaining forever dark and lifeless, containing nothing more complex or interesting than nearly uniform gas. If, on the other hand, the dark-energy density were reduced enough to be significantly negative, then our universe would have stopped expanding ,recollapsing in a cataclysmic big crunch before any life had time to evolve.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 If the electormagnetic force were weakened by a mere 4%, then the sun would immediately explode as its hydrogen fused into diprotons, an otherwise nonexistent kind of neutron free helium.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The interesting point here is that we could have used this fine-tuning of our solar system to argue that different solar systems should exist even before any were discovered. Using the exact same logic, we can use the observed fine-tunings of our universe to argue for the existence of different universes. The only difference is whether the other predicted entities are observalbe or not, but this difference doesn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t weaken the argument, since it never enters into the logic.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Our glactic address- eight planets whose orbits ar 1.84, 2.51, 4.33, 12.7, 24.7, 51.1, 76.5 larger than the innermost planet.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The nine masses that weve managed to measure for the fermion particles look ratther random, as some multiverse models predict, suggesting that we\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ll never manage to calculate them from first principles. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Guth - when we discover an object in nature, the scientific thing to do is look for a mechanism that created it. Cars are created by car factoreies, rabbits and solar systems are created from gravitational collapse in ginat molecular clouds. So its reasonable to assume that our universe was created by some sort of universe creation mechanism. All other mechanisms naturally produce *many* copies of whatever they create; a cosmos containing only one car, one rabbit, one solar system would seem quite contrived. In the same vein, its arguably more antural for the correct universe-creation mechansim to create many universes rather than just one.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Plato in his dialogue Timaeus that the four basic elements postulated at the time consisted of four kinds of atoms, and that these atmos were invisibly small mathematical objexts: cubes, ocosahedra, octahedra and tetrahedra, respectively. For example, the sharp corners of the tetrahedron explained why fire was painful, that the ball like shape of the icosahedron explained water\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s ability to flow, and that the unique ability of cubes to be compactly stacked explained earth\u8217\'92s silidity. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Godld is produced when a star dies in a supernova explosion so vilent and rare that it, during a fraction of a second, releases about as much energy as all other stars in our observable universe combined. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a particle - its an element of an irreducible representation of the symmetry group of the Lagrangian.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Bryce DeWitt\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 complained to Everett that he just didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t feel like he was constantlyh splitting into parallel versions of himself. He told me that everett had responded with a question: \u8220\'93Do you feel like you\u8217\'92re orbiting the sun at thirty kilometers per second?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Quantum effects decohere very quickly in brain\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s architecture.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Cosmological interpretation of quantum mechanics. Here we interpret the wavefunction for an object as desccribing not some funky im aginary ensemble of possibilites for w3hat the object might be doing but rather the actual spatial collection of identical copies of the object that exist in our infite sapce. Moreover, quantum uncertainty that you experience simply reflects your inability to self-locate in the level I multiverse, I.I. to know which of your infinitely many copies throughout space is the one having your subjective perceptions.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Each of us has our own personal inner reality, perceived from the subjective perspective of our own position, orientation and state of mind, and distorted by our personal congintive biases: in your inner reality, dreams are real and the world turns upside down when you stand on your head. In contrast, the consesus reality is shared. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ball was designed by humans, but it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s quite analogous for composite objects that aren\u8217\'92t man-made, such as molecules, rocks, and stars: inventing words for htem is convenient both for saving time, and for providing concepts or so-called shorthand abstractions in terms to understand the world more intuitively. Although useful such words are all optional baggage: ofr example, ive used the word star, but you could in principle replace eveery occurrence of it by a definintion in terms of its building blocks, say, gravitationaly bound clump of about 10^57 atoms, some of which are undergoing nuclear fusion. In other words, nature contains many types of entities that are almost begging to be maned. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 You can often predict the existence of such name-worthy entitites mathematically, from the equations governing their parts. In this way, the whole lego-like hierarchy of structures can be predicted, from elementary particls to atoms to molecules, and what we humans add are merely catchy names for the objects at each level. For example, if you solve the Schrodinger equation for five or fewer quarks, it turns out that there are only two fairly stable ways for them to be arrange: either as a clump of two up quarks and a down quark or as a clump of two down quarks and an up quark, and we humans have added the baggage of calling these two types of clumps protons and neutrons for convenience. Similarly, if you apply the schrodinger equation to such clumps, it turns out that there are only 257 stable ways for them to be assembled together. We humans have added the baggage of calling these proton/neutron assemblies \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93atomic nuclei\u8221\'94, and have also invented specific names for each kidn: hydrogen, helium, etc. The schrodinger equation also lets you calculate all the ways of putting atoms together into larger objects, but this time, there turn out to be so many different stable objects taht its inconvenient to name them all, instead, we\u8217\'92ve just named important classes of object( such as molecules and crustals) and the most common interesting objects in each class (e.g. water, graphite, diamond).}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Lee Smolin, Time Reborn - not only is change real,  but that indeed time may be the only thing that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s real. Julian barbour, the end of time.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The spacetime braind that corresponds to the mind is the most beautifully complex type of pattern we\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve encountered in the universe.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 His guess: no spectator is needed, because your consciousness basically is your reality model. I think that consciousness is the way information feels when being processed in certain complex ways.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The neuroscientist Giulio Tononi has made an intriguing proposal for how to quantify the required interconnectedness, described in the publications by Koch and Tonioni,,, the core isdea is that for an information processing system to be conscious, it needs to be integrated into a unified whole that can\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t be decomposed into nearly independent parts. This means that all parts need to com pute jointly with lots of information about each other\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 otherwise there would be more than one independent consciousness. This is closely linked to the so-called redundancy and error-correcting codes used in bar codes, hard drives, tec. You use more bits than the minimum needed, which encode your information in a clever collective way such that none of your information is lost even if you lose any modest fraction of your bits. Our brain appears to use a similarly redundant architecture, since it doesnt seem to depend critically on any single neuron.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Science: Make predictions from assumptions. Compare observations with predictions, update assumptions. Repeat}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The measure problem Physics in crisis}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -essentially inflation predicts nothing because it predicts everything.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 A part of space that experienced its big bang more recently will be hotter, because it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s had less time to cool off, so finding ourselves in a relatively cool universe is highly unlikely and we have a coolness problem: when I worked out the probability of measuring the cosmic microwave background temperature to be less than three degrees above absolute zero, I got 10^-10^56, so when the COBE-satellite measured this temperature to be 2.725 Kelvin, this measurement ruled out our inflation based story with 99.99999\u8230\'85999% confidence, with a hundred million trillion trillion trillion n ines after the decimal point. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We asked a reasonable question about wwhat the typeical observer should expect to measure\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 we picked the particular example of the cosmic mmicrowave temperature. Because we considered eternal inflation, we analyzed a spacetime containing many observer moments measuring many different temperatures, so we couldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t predict just one unique answer, mere probabilities for different temperature ranges. This, per se, isn\u8217\'92t the end of the world. Rather, the problem was that the probabilities we computed told us that what we in fact observe is ridiculously unlikely, so that the underlying theory is ruled out. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The probabilites are simply the fractions of all observer moments in our reference class that measure various temperatures. If there are only five such observer moments and they observe 1,2,5,10 and 12 degrees above absolute zero, then the fraction measuring less than three is two out of five, 2/5 = 40%\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 easy! But what if, as eternal inflation predicts, there are infinitely many such observer moments, and the fraction measuring less than three degrees is infinity divided by infinity? How do we make sense of that?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mathematicians have developed an elegeant scheme called taking limits, which in many cases cna make sense of infinity/infinity. For example, what fraction of all the counting numbers 1,2,3\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85 are even? There are infinitely many numbers and infinitely many of them are even, so the fraction is infinity/infinity. But if we count only the first N numbers, we get as ensible anser that depends slightly on our counting cutoff N. If we keep increasing N, we find that the fraction jiggles around less and less as N grows. If we now take the limit where N approaches inifnity, we get a well-defined answer that doesn\u8217\'92t depend on N at all: exactly half of the numbers are even.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This seems like a snesible answer, but inifinities are treac herous: the fraction of the numbers that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s even depends ont the order in which we count them! If we instead order the numbers 1,2,4,3,6,7,5,10,12,7, and so on, then the same limit scheme gives the answer that 2/3 of the numbers are even. Because as we proceed down this list of numbers, we encounter two even numbers for every odd number. We didn\u8217\'92t cheat, since all even and odd numbers eventaullly show up in our list; we merely reordered them. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Analogously, the fraction of all the infinitely many observers in spaetime who make a particular observation depends on the roder in which you count them! We cosmologists use the term }{\f2\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 measure}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  to refer to an observer-moment ordering scheme, or, more generally, to a method for calculating probablitiles from annoying infinities. The crazy probabilities I computed for the coolness problem corresponded to a praticular measrue, and most of my colleagues guess that the problem isn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t wiht inflation but with the measure: somehow, it appeares flawed to talk about hte reference class of all observer moments at a fixed time.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The last few years have seen an avalanche of interesting papers proposing alternative measures. I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ts proven remarkably difficult to find one that works with eternal inflation: some measures flunk the coolness problem; others fail by predicting that you\u8217\'92re a boltzmann brain; yet others predict that we should see our sky warped by giant black holes. Alex Vilenkin recently told me that he was getting disheartened: a few years ago, he\u8217\'92d hoped that only one measure would avoid all these pitfalls, and that it would be so simple and elegant that it convinced us all. Instead, we now have an umber of different measures that appear to give different but reasonable predictions, with no obvious way to choose between them. If the probabilities we predict depend on the measure we assume, and we can assume a measure giving almost any answer we want, then we really haven\u8217\'92t predicted anything at all.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 As I see it, inflation has logically self-destructed. We started taking inflation seriously because it made correct predictions: it predicted that typical observers should measure space around them to be flat rahter than curved (the flatness problem); they should measure their cosmic microwave background temperature to be similar in all directiosn (the horizon problem); they should measure a power spectrum similar to what the WMAP satellite saw, etc. But then it predicted infinitely many observers measruing different things with probabilites depending on a measure that we don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t know. Which in turn means that inflation, strictly speaking, isn\u8217\'92t predicting anything at all about what typical observers should see. All predictions are revoked. }
